1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,160
So he actually did an experiment with baseball cards,

2
00:00:03,160 --> 00:00:04,680
where he tried seeing whether there was

3
00:00:04,680 --> 00:00:06,320
a dominant effect for baseball cards among

4
00:00:06,320 --> 00:00:09,680
professional baseball card traders and found that there is not.

5
00:00:09,680 --> 00:00:12,840
So people basically are buying and selling for a living,

6
00:00:12,840 --> 00:00:15,960
seem to act in accordance with the simple standard theory.

7
00:00:15,960 --> 00:00:19,480
Even though if you were just going to give everyone this class of baseball card,

8
00:00:19,480 --> 00:00:20,760
a lot of you probably would say,

9
00:00:20,760 --> 00:00:22,160
well, this is my baseball card now,

10
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:24,600
I'm going to keep my special baseball card.

11
00:00:24,600 --> 00:00:26,880
If I were to give you a whole box of the baseball cards,

12
00:00:26,880 --> 00:00:28,840
you might want to keep one and then sell the rest back and

13
00:00:28,840 --> 00:00:31,960
be happy just to get rid of the weight.

14
00:00:31,960 --> 00:00:36,120
All right. So questions about this stuff?

15
00:00:36,120 --> 00:00:39,520
Yeah. So to me, it was when I was doing economics in the family that I

16
00:00:39,520 --> 00:00:43,240
realized that the endowment effect has huge effects in the world.

17
00:00:43,240 --> 00:00:47,040
And just the way that unplanned pregnancy so often,

18
00:00:47,040 --> 00:00:49,320
not just lead to someone reluctantly,

19
00:00:49,320 --> 00:00:50,800
unhappily having a child,

20
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:53,400
but actually to being super glad about it.

21
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:58,080
That's pretty odd in terms of any kind of normal economic theory.

22
00:00:58,080 --> 00:01:03,600
And yet it is a very strong part of human experience that this person that

23
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:08,000
you totally didn't want and you act and like,

24
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:10,200
when you find out pregnancy, my life is over.

25
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:12,800
Then three years later, instead of saying my life is over,

26
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:15,400
I can't imagine my life without you.

27
00:01:15,400 --> 00:01:19,040
And again, this is not like this happened once in a thousand times.

28
00:01:19,040 --> 00:01:21,840
This is a very common occurrence.

29
00:01:21,840 --> 00:01:24,760
So pretty hard to work,

30
00:01:24,760 --> 00:01:28,960
hard to reconcile with any normal economic theory without adding in

31
00:01:28,960 --> 00:01:33,000
just the very fact that this child is Korea,

32
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:35,520
like becomes yours through this process.

33
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:38,200
My baby. This is my baby.

34
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:39,920
And now I love my baby.

35
00:01:39,920 --> 00:01:41,360
Like your baby's a jerk.

36
00:01:41,360 --> 00:01:43,120
It's one of the worst babies ever met.

37
00:01:43,120 --> 00:01:44,720
I love him anyway.

38
00:01:44,720 --> 00:01:48,640
Everything about him. I love his lies.

39
00:01:48,640 --> 00:01:50,760
I love his tantrums.

40
00:01:50,760 --> 00:01:56,440
All right. So that seems like a pretty strong and clear case.

41
00:01:56,440 --> 00:01:59,800
All right. Questions, questions.

42
00:01:59,800 --> 00:02:02,800
All right. And moving on.

43
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:05,640
All right. Selfishness and cooperation.

44
00:02:05,640 --> 00:02:09,760
All right. So how many times have you heard the Adam Smith quotes?

45
00:02:09,760 --> 00:02:12,560
Told to you just since you started your PhD,

46
00:02:12,560 --> 00:02:14,680
not from benevolence of the baker make his bread,

47
00:02:14,680 --> 00:02:17,000
but from his own regard to his own self-interest.

48
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:19,080
Right? Economists love this quote.

49
00:02:19,080 --> 00:02:21,400
And yet if you look many of the same economists,

50
00:02:21,400 --> 00:02:23,400
you say so economists think people are selfish.

51
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:26,880
They'll go, no, no, I just think that maximizing utility function.

52
00:02:26,880 --> 00:02:30,360
A Smith quote is not about maximizing utility function.

53
00:02:30,360 --> 00:02:34,280
In, if you had said bakers bake bread out of benevolence,

54
00:02:34,280 --> 00:02:36,520
is that maximizing utility function?

55
00:02:36,520 --> 00:02:41,120
Sure. So why is that an important and insightful quote?

56
00:02:41,120 --> 00:02:43,760
And why do economists keep coming back to it?

57
00:02:43,760 --> 00:02:47,040
I don't keep repeating that quote.

58
00:02:47,040 --> 00:02:50,240
Shows that altruism isn't necessary.

59
00:02:50,240 --> 00:02:53,400
It's sufficient but not necessary to produce the goods.

60
00:02:53,400 --> 00:02:55,520
I mean, but like Smith didn't say,

61
00:02:55,520 --> 00:02:58,000
benevolence is not necessary to bake the bread.

62
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:01,160
He actually said benevolence doesn't bake the bread.

63
00:03:01,160 --> 00:03:03,960
It's self-interest that makes the bread.

64
00:03:03,960 --> 00:03:07,000
So, it tends to be people are selfish.

65
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:08,920
Yeah. This is a,

66
00:03:08,920 --> 00:03:12,160
this, there's no reason to bring that quote up,

67
00:03:12,160 --> 00:03:15,080
unless you're making a point about how people are selfish,

68
00:03:15,080 --> 00:03:17,120
and that selfishness leads to good outcomes.

69
00:03:17,120 --> 00:03:18,800
But you know, there's no point saying

70
00:03:18,800 --> 00:03:19,960
selfishness leads to good outcomes,

71
00:03:19,960 --> 00:03:22,800
unless you also are saying that selfishness is common.

72
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:25,400
Right. And of course is underestimated.

73
00:03:25,400 --> 00:03:27,880
So anyway, economists in

74
00:03:27,880 --> 00:03:31,080
practice almost always assume human selfishness.

75
00:03:31,080 --> 00:03:33,080
Again, there are special narrow cases

76
00:03:33,080 --> 00:03:34,600
where they have a specific justification,

77
00:03:34,600 --> 00:03:36,760
like if you're doing economics of parenting,

78
00:03:36,760 --> 00:03:39,280
then we usually say parents love their kids.

79
00:03:39,280 --> 00:03:41,120
Or if you're doing economics of charity,

80
00:03:41,120 --> 00:03:43,520
then there's probably some charity on the pulse.

81
00:03:43,520 --> 00:03:45,080
But for all the other cases,

82
00:03:45,080 --> 00:03:46,800
it's totally normal for economists

83
00:03:46,800 --> 00:03:48,600
to just take selfishness for granted.

84
00:03:48,600 --> 00:03:50,600
And it's only when someone starts whining

85
00:03:50,600 --> 00:03:52,840
about your assuming selfishness that they start coming up,

86
00:03:52,840 --> 00:03:55,120
and start saying, no, no, it's just a utility function.

87
00:03:55,120 --> 00:03:56,640
And again, normally when the person who

88
00:03:56,640 --> 00:03:58,760
complains about the selfishness leaves the room,

89
00:03:58,760 --> 00:04:01,240
then what do the economists go back to doing?

90
00:04:01,240 --> 00:04:04,040
Yeah. Assuming everyone, assuming people are selfish.

91
00:04:04,040 --> 00:04:07,360
All right. So anyway. Now, pure theorists are

92
00:04:07,360 --> 00:04:09,120
the ones who usually want to go and say,

93
00:04:09,120 --> 00:04:11,120
I don't assume selfishness at all.

94
00:04:11,120 --> 00:04:13,840
No, I just assume that there's a well-earned utility function.

95
00:04:13,840 --> 00:04:15,960
Which is that people care about

96
00:04:15,960 --> 00:04:17,160
only their own endowments,

97
00:04:17,160 --> 00:04:19,160
their own material possessions.

98
00:04:19,160 --> 00:04:22,720
That sounds kind of like a selfish person.

99
00:04:22,720 --> 00:04:25,520
All right. So anyway, pure theorists

100
00:04:25,520 --> 00:04:28,360
often will run away from the Smith quotation.

101
00:04:28,360 --> 00:04:32,560
But whenever we apply economics in the real world,

102
00:04:32,560 --> 00:04:37,920
selfishness is almost always right there, front and center.

103
00:04:37,920 --> 00:04:40,080
And again, not just some kind of

104
00:04:40,080 --> 00:04:42,520
everything is selfish no matter what you do.

105
00:04:42,520 --> 00:04:44,080
But even like narrow selfish.

106
00:04:44,080 --> 00:04:46,160
Like Smith, if you were to say,

107
00:04:46,160 --> 00:04:49,640
well look, if you like baking bread, that's selfish too.

108
00:04:49,640 --> 00:04:51,760
Is that what Smith is getting at?

109
00:04:51,760 --> 00:04:55,240
No. He's using self-interest in the normal sense.

110
00:04:55,240 --> 00:04:56,760
According to which a person who bakes

111
00:04:56,760 --> 00:04:58,480
bread and gives it to strangers

112
00:04:58,480 --> 00:05:00,600
because he loves everybody is not selfish.

113
00:05:00,600 --> 00:05:03,600
But a person who bakes bread to make money is selfish.

114
00:05:03,600 --> 00:05:05,880
And you say, well, if they're both for

115
00:05:05,880 --> 00:05:07,480
your own personal satisfaction,

116
00:05:07,480 --> 00:05:09,400
why is one more selfish than the other?

117
00:05:09,400 --> 00:05:13,560
I say the easy answer is because of the English language.

118
00:05:13,560 --> 00:05:15,720
Because that's what the words mean.

119
00:05:15,720 --> 00:05:18,040
Like a person who just loves other people

120
00:05:18,040 --> 00:05:20,240
and gets super happy helping orphans.

121
00:05:20,240 --> 00:05:23,200
We don't say this is a selfish orphan lover.

122
00:05:23,200 --> 00:05:27,240
We say this is a nice person who likes helping orphans.

123
00:05:27,240 --> 00:05:29,040
So you're only doing it because you like

124
00:05:29,040 --> 00:05:32,360
helping orphans for free, jerk.

125
00:05:32,360 --> 00:05:35,760
No. That's the very definition of altruism.

126
00:05:35,760 --> 00:05:39,080
If you like, at least it is one perfectly fine form.

127
00:05:39,080 --> 00:05:40,720
If there's someone who does it out of

128
00:05:40,720 --> 00:05:42,480
a sense of duty even though they hate it,

129
00:05:42,480 --> 00:05:44,440
you might say, oh, I find they're unselfish too.

130
00:05:44,440 --> 00:05:47,000
But even there, you say, well, who's more unselfish?

131
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:49,240
The person who helps the orphans out of love or the person

132
00:05:49,240 --> 00:05:51,080
helps them because they feel like

133
00:05:51,080 --> 00:05:54,040
Manuel Kant proved they have to do it.

134
00:05:54,040 --> 00:05:56,240
At least it's an interesting,

135
00:05:56,240 --> 00:05:57,680
you're like, well, I don't know which one is

136
00:05:57,680 --> 00:06:01,080
the more unselfish according to normal English.

137
00:06:01,200 --> 00:06:05,720
Okay. So when economists use economics to explain the world,

138
00:06:05,720 --> 00:06:09,000
we normally do assume human selfishness in a narrow sense.

139
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:12,120
You ever heard of Don McGraw's story,

140
00:06:12,120 --> 00:06:13,840
a question about kidneys?

141
00:06:13,840 --> 00:06:16,800
Comes down to this. Can I have your kidney for free?

142
00:06:16,800 --> 00:06:17,520
No.

143
00:06:17,520 --> 00:06:20,640
What if I need it? But I need your kidney for free. Can I just have it?

144
00:06:20,640 --> 00:06:21,640
I need my kidney.

145
00:06:21,640 --> 00:06:22,360
Yes.

146
00:06:22,360 --> 00:06:23,160
What?

147
00:06:23,160 --> 00:06:24,200
I need my kidney.

148
00:06:24,200 --> 00:06:25,600
Yeah. Well, actually,

149
00:06:25,600 --> 00:06:27,520
a doctor would tell you that you only need one.

150
00:06:27,520 --> 00:06:30,960
You've got two. Just like elective surgery.

151
00:06:30,960 --> 00:06:31,720
It's no big deal.

152
00:06:31,720 --> 00:06:34,480
Like one in 10,000 death rates,

153
00:06:34,480 --> 00:06:36,080
it's like an auto accident per year,

154
00:06:36,080 --> 00:06:37,720
and like I'd like a kidney.

155
00:06:37,720 --> 00:06:43,400
So very few people are inclined to give a stranger their kidney for free.

156
00:06:43,400 --> 00:06:45,800
And they would say, well,

157
00:06:45,800 --> 00:06:48,920
like this would really turn the recipient's life around.

158
00:06:48,920 --> 00:06:51,440
I think I'll still won't do it.

159
00:06:51,440 --> 00:06:53,960
It isn't a normal human reaction.

160
00:06:53,960 --> 00:06:55,960
There are a few people like Dylan Matthews and

161
00:06:55,960 --> 00:06:59,320
Vox when he gave his kidney away to a total stranger.

162
00:06:59,320 --> 00:07:03,280
So this is a case where someone says he's a utilitarian,

163
00:07:03,280 --> 00:07:06,800
and he appears to act like that is very consistent with doing that.

164
00:07:06,800 --> 00:07:09,120
But most people aren't like that.

165
00:07:09,120 --> 00:07:11,320
Okay. All right.

166
00:07:11,320 --> 00:07:14,320
Now, a slight variation that economists have sometimes brought in,

167
00:07:14,320 --> 00:07:16,400
especially when they're doing economics with family,

168
00:07:16,400 --> 00:07:23,280
is to go over biology and talk about inclusive fitness.

169
00:07:23,280 --> 00:07:26,760
All right. So how many of you here have ever read The Selfish Gene?

170
00:07:26,760 --> 00:07:28,880
Where's your dog in this? A few people.

171
00:07:28,880 --> 00:07:32,000
All right. So this is

172
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:35,480
a simple Darwinian theory about who you really love,

173
00:07:35,480 --> 00:07:37,960
and who you are just using.

174
00:07:37,960 --> 00:07:40,640
And the story is, well,

175
00:07:40,640 --> 00:07:43,760
suppose that cavemen were willing to go and

176
00:07:43,760 --> 00:07:46,320
give their food away for free to total strangers.

177
00:07:46,320 --> 00:07:51,160
All right. So what's this going to do to their number of surviving descendants?

178
00:07:51,160 --> 00:07:53,880
It's going to hurt it. Right?

179
00:07:53,880 --> 00:07:57,080
On the other hand, suppose you had a caveman who had his own flesh and blood,

180
00:07:57,080 --> 00:07:59,280
his own biological children, and said,

181
00:07:59,280 --> 00:08:02,040
meh, you're never going to do anything for me.

182
00:08:02,040 --> 00:08:03,360
Right? Dead and no way going.

183
00:08:03,360 --> 00:08:06,960
What would happen to the children of those cavemen?

184
00:08:06,960 --> 00:08:09,880
Also, not many of them survive.

185
00:08:09,880 --> 00:08:13,360
So what is selected by evolution?

186
00:08:13,360 --> 00:08:16,400
It is to not care much about strangers,

187
00:08:16,400 --> 00:08:19,520
but to care a lot about your own biological relatives.

188
00:08:19,520 --> 00:08:22,960
Right? And in particular, inclusive fitness says that

189
00:08:22,960 --> 00:08:28,080
the genetic equilibrium is one where the genes are maximized.

190
00:08:28,080 --> 00:08:30,520
And this occurs when you go and value

191
00:08:30,520 --> 00:08:33,680
other people based upon how genetically related they are to you.

192
00:08:33,680 --> 00:08:36,160
So basically this means if you can,

193
00:08:36,160 --> 00:08:37,320
so each of your children,

194
00:08:37,320 --> 00:08:39,280
you share half their genes.

195
00:08:39,280 --> 00:08:43,160
So it says you'd be different between your own life and two kids.

196
00:08:43,160 --> 00:08:46,400
Or your own life and eight cousins.

197
00:08:46,400 --> 00:08:49,840
Right? Or your own life and four half-siblings.

198
00:08:49,840 --> 00:08:51,960
Sound right? All right.

199
00:08:51,960 --> 00:08:56,000
That is what inclusive fitness is all about.

200
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:57,840
It does explain a lot.

201
00:08:57,840 --> 00:08:58,960
If you think about it more,

202
00:08:58,960 --> 00:09:01,040
you will notice some anomalies to that too.

203
00:09:01,040 --> 00:09:03,480
But anyway, there are some economists who have added

204
00:09:03,480 --> 00:09:05,240
an inclusive fitness and say, well,

205
00:09:05,240 --> 00:09:09,040
when someone goes and is generally selfish,

206
00:09:09,040 --> 00:09:12,240
but they lavish stuff on their own biological kids,

207
00:09:12,240 --> 00:09:15,400
we're still going to call them selfish, broadly speaking.

208
00:09:15,400 --> 00:09:17,960
And again, I would say this is not so weird.

209
00:09:17,960 --> 00:09:21,520
So imagine, you know, think about all the TV shows where there's an axe murderer.

210
00:09:21,520 --> 00:09:23,560
And as usual, the axe murderer's dad is

211
00:09:23,560 --> 00:09:27,880
a billionaire who buys him the best legal defense he could possibly get.

212
00:09:27,880 --> 00:09:29,800
And you go and confront the family,

213
00:09:29,800 --> 00:09:31,960
the victim is going to confront the billionaire and say,

214
00:09:31,960 --> 00:09:35,560
you're a horrible person trying to put your axe murdering son back on the street.

215
00:09:35,560 --> 00:09:37,920
And then the billionaire says,

216
00:09:37,920 --> 00:09:40,240
I love my son.

217
00:09:40,240 --> 00:09:42,920
All right. The point of this is,

218
00:09:42,920 --> 00:09:46,320
are we supposed to think of the billionaire trying to put his axe murdering son back on

219
00:09:46,320 --> 00:09:51,280
the street as being a selfish jerk or being a wonderful loving parent?

220
00:09:51,520 --> 00:09:54,760
Normally think of them as a selfish jerk because sure,

221
00:09:54,760 --> 00:09:56,160
they do love their own kid,

222
00:09:56,160 --> 00:10:00,760
but they don't care at all about all the people that their kid is going to murder with his axe.

223
00:10:00,760 --> 00:10:05,560
So that is a sense in which you might say that inclusive fitness,

224
00:10:05,560 --> 00:10:08,400
it's still basically in the same general realm of human selfishness.

225
00:10:08,400 --> 00:10:12,200
Or you know, you found out that some guy in the mafia gave his daughter a really nice car,

226
00:10:12,200 --> 00:10:15,200
would you say, oh my God, I've totally rethinked my view of this man?

227
00:10:15,200 --> 00:10:19,680
No, it's like, well yeah, well that's just what you'd expect a mafia boss to be like.

228
00:10:19,680 --> 00:10:23,120
Murders his enemies, but buys his daughter a great car.

229
00:10:23,120 --> 00:10:27,280
It doesn't change my view about him as a villain at all.

230
00:10:27,640 --> 00:10:33,680
Okay. Now, as you might remember from week six,

231
00:10:33,680 --> 00:10:37,080
when there is repeated play in a game,

232
00:10:37,080 --> 00:10:41,440
then totally selfish non-relatives will still cooperate.

233
00:10:41,440 --> 00:10:43,760
Why? Because of reciprocity.

234
00:10:43,760 --> 00:10:46,760
Right? You're cooperating in order to get something.

235
00:10:46,760 --> 00:10:52,280
So there's that. So mere cooperation is not good evidence against human selfishness.

236
00:10:52,280 --> 00:10:55,480
You need to focus on cooperation where one person gets

237
00:10:55,480 --> 00:11:00,440
an endless stream of free stuff and the other one knows they're not going to get anything back.

238
00:11:00,440 --> 00:11:05,720
That is the clear case where it's not just implicit trade.

239
00:11:05,720 --> 00:11:08,240
Right? Whereas most relationships,

240
00:11:08,240 --> 00:11:10,640
even once you think about between friends,

241
00:11:10,640 --> 00:11:14,080
they fit the model of implicit trade pretty well.

242
00:11:14,080 --> 00:11:19,400
Like think about cases where there's two friends and one of them always helps the other one,

243
00:11:19,400 --> 00:11:21,200
and the other one never reciprocates.

244
00:11:21,200 --> 00:11:23,440
How stable are friendships like that,

245
00:11:23,440 --> 00:11:26,040
where it's totally one way or like that?

246
00:11:26,040 --> 00:11:29,520
So generally going forward, those seem to be very stable friendships.

247
00:11:29,520 --> 00:11:32,320
Stable friendships are ones where there's reciprocity,

248
00:11:32,320 --> 00:11:34,160
where each side is able to help the other.

249
00:11:34,160 --> 00:11:37,280
And of course, it doesn't need to be to balance out every single day.

250
00:11:37,280 --> 00:11:42,360
But if 10 years have gone by and the one person who gives has never gotten anything back,

251
00:11:42,360 --> 00:11:46,640
that's a case where you'll start to wonder what is going on there.

252
00:11:46,640 --> 00:11:53,200
Right? All right. So questions about that? Yes.

253
00:11:53,200 --> 00:11:55,560
